/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  transport02 -- CHAPMAN-ENSKOG FROM SVEHLA'S CONSTANTS, AND THE FIRST REAL
  SPREAD.

  NOTHING IN THIS CASE IS MEASURED, except in the one sense the anchors
  state.  The seven Lennard-Jones pairs (sigma, eps/k) on the records in
  constant/components/ are Svehla's LEAST-SQUARES FITS to viscosities
  measured before 1962 (his Table I(a) method 1, one row per gas, cited by
  report page in each record's own provenance block); Choupo holds none of
  those measurements and compares with none.  What the run shows:

    * TWO models per gas family, so the spread column is a DISAGREEMENT and
      not the 0.00 % of transport01: Chung (corresponding states from Tc,
      Pc, omega -- non-polar truncation, Vc estimated, both announced) beside
      Chapman-Enskog (kinetic theory over the fitted sigma, eps/k); plain
      Eucken beside the modified Eucken (Svehla eq. (2), the internal modes
      weighted 1.32).  Water is where the two viscosities part furthest:
      Chung's non-polar form against a pair FITTED to steam data.
    * Argon is the control: monatomic, so Eucken and the modified Eucken
      coincide to the extent the record's Cp polynomial returns 5R/2 (it
      does, to four figures at 300 K; lambda'' is the residual) and the
      polyatomic flag stays silent.
    * Every Chapman-Enskog component is ANNOUNCED with the ORIGIN of both
      constants (FITTED to measured transport data, Svehla method 1) and
      the page they were read from; an estimated pair would say ESTIMATED
      in the same place.  The window verdict is T* = kT/eps against the
      Neufeld fit's 0.3 <= T* <= 100; water at 300 K sits at T* = 0.37,
      inside, near the low edge.
    * The comparison point is 300 K, 1 bar -- the 300 K row of Svehla's
      Table III -- so a reader can set the printed viscosities beside his
      (report pages 31 Ar, 56 CH4, 58 CO2, 74 H2, 75 H2O, 90 N2, 98 O2) and
      see the residual the anchor row measures: the Neufeld 1972 fit of the
      collision integral against the Hirschfelder 1954 tables he
      interpolated, 0.1-0.8 % across these gases, 1.8 % for water.

  THE ANCHORS.  ChapmanEnskog: ARITHMETIC -- with Svehla's own M, sigma,
  eps/k for N2 the engine returns his printed eta at 300 K (report page 90)
  within a DECLARED 1 % (0.40 % observed); it checks the constant 26.693,
  the unit chain and the shared collision-integral home, nothing about any
  gas.  modifiedEucken: THEORY -- a monatomic gas must give exactly
  (15/4) R mu / M -- after reproducing Svehla's printed N2 conductivity
  from his own eta and Cp/R through eq. (2).  The bench prints the kind.

  WHAT THIS CASE DOES NOT DO.  It does not say which viscosity is right for
  water or for anything else, and it does not compare with a measurement.
  The records are CASE-LOCAL (adopted, on the edwards02 precedent): the
  public catalogue carries no lennardJones block until Vitor promotes one;
  bin/curate/propose_lennard_jones.py drafts them into data/local/ from the
  committed transcription bin/curate/svehla1962/.  Binary diffusivity by
  Chapman-Enskog is NOT here: the Omega(1,1)* fit is not citable from any
  source in the tree.  Record:
  docs/design/transport-correlations-as-objects.md, section 8.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name     bench;
        type     transportBench;
        //  300 K, 1 bar: Svehla's own 300 K row, so every printed viscosity
        //  can be read beside his Table III page for that gas.
        compare
        {
            T   300 K;
            P   1 bar;
        }
    }
);
